⸻

One important thing I changed from your previous version

I didn’t make every field its own test case anymore.

For example, instead of:

* Verify Project Name
* Verify Transaction
* Verify Rationale
* Verify Responsible Person
* Verify Accountable Executive
* Verify MT Sponsor
* Verify Business/Function
* Verify CFCR RFOs

I use:

Verify Corporate Action category fields
Review Project Name, Transaction, Rationale, Responsible Person, Accountable Executive, MT Sponsor, Business/Function and CFCR RFOs.

And the Expected Result explicitly says:

All Corporate Action fields are displayed with the correct case values.

That’s perfectly valid UAT granularity because it is one logical validation of the category’s displayed data, while still making every field being tested explicit.

The same principle is used for NICRA, Other, New Product/Product Change, Details Panel and Export.

The final sheet structure I’d use

PM

1. Initiative
2. Landing Page
3. Landing Page Export
4. Workflow
5. Workflow Export

RFO

1. Landing Page
2. Landing Page Export
3. Workflow
4. Workflow Export

And columns:

Test Case ID | Test Scenario | Test Steps | Expected Result

If your existing Investment workbook has Preconditions / Test Data / Status / Actual Result / Evidence, add those columns after Expected Result, but the four above are the actual test-case content.
